+# AGENTS.md
+
+This file orients a coding agent working in this checkout of Wine.
+
+## What this repo is
+
+This is a fork of upstream Wine (`https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine`),
+maintained for [wine-assess](https://github.com/kasm-technologies) (repo:
+`~/dev/kasm/projects/wine-assess`) — the project that assesses whether Windows
+apps run under Wine, and remediates when they don't. Most of the time Wine
+gaps are worked around with levers (Windows-version flips, DLL overrides,
+winetricks verbs). This fork exists for the residue that needs an actual code
+change: patches Wine doesn't (yet) carry, adopted from the community so a
+specific class of app works.
+
+It is **not** a general-purpose Wine distribution. Every patch here is here
+because some app wine-assess assessed needed it. Don't add a patch
+speculatively; add it when an assessment names the gap.
+
+## Remotes and branches (current reality)
+
+```
+origin    https://gitlab.com/kasm-technologies/labs-sandbox/wine.git   (fetch+push) — our fork, hosted
+upstream  https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine                          (fetch+push) — read-only in practice
+```
+
+`origin` is **our own hosted fork** (created 2026-09-13, resolves `OI-027`'s
+hosting question). `upstream` is the real Wine project — we fetch from it to
+pick up new releases and to review what's changed before a patch-branch
+rebase, but we never push to it and never commit directly to a branch that
+mirrors it.
+
+Branches:
+
+- `master` — mirrors `upstream/master`. Never commit here; only ever
+  fast-forwarded from `upstream`, then pushed to `origin` to keep our hosted
+  copy current.
+- `patch/<topic>` — one branch per patch set (see below).
+- `kasm/main` — the integration branch: the upstream tag we ship, plus every
+  `patch/<topic>` branch cherry-picked on top, in a fixed order. **This is what
+  gets built.** Tagged `kasm-wine-<upstream-tag>-p<N>`; `N` bumps whenever the
+  patch set changes on the same upstream base.
+- `upgrade/wine-<tag>` — created only while moving to a new upstream release
+  (see below). Not long-lived; folded back into `kasm/main` once the move is
+  done.
+
+All of the above live on `origin` (our fork). Nothing is ever pushed to
+`upstream`.
+
+## The patch-branch convention
+
+1. **One topic branch per patch set**, `patch/<topic>`, branched from the
+   upstream release tag we currently ship. A topic branch holds only that
+   set's commits — one commit per source patch — each with a `Source:`
+   trailer (URL or repo path, plus author lineage) so provenance survives a
+   rebase.
+2. **`kasm/main` never carries a patch directly.** It is rebuilt by cherry-
+   picking every topic branch onto the upstream tag, in order. If a cherry-pick
+   conflicts, fix it **on the topic branch**, never by hand-editing `kasm/main`.
+3. **A patch that upstream absorbs gets dropped**, not carried forward dead.
+   This is the part worth doing deliberately rather than skipping under time
+   pressure — see the next section.
+
+## Moving to a new upstream release (the reconciliation step)
+
+This is the step that's easy to skip and shouldn't be: don't just rebase every
+topic branch onto the new tag and declare victory. For **each** `patch/<topic>`
+branch, before rebasing it:
+
+1. Fetch `upstream` and read its own log/changelog for the files and functions
+   the patch touches between the old base and the new tag:
+   `git fetch upstream && git log --oneline <old-tag>..<new-tag> -- <paths the patch touches>`.
+2. Decide, per patch commit, one of:
+   - **Still needed, ports clean or with minor conflict fixes** — rebase it,
+     resolve conflicts on the topic branch, note what changed in the port.
+   - **Upstream absorbed it** (same fix landed independently, possibly
+     differently implemented) — **drop it** from the topic branch and record
+     that it was dropped and why (upstream commit/PR if you can find one).
+     Don't keep a patch alive just because it still applies textually if
+     upstream's own code now does the same thing a different way — check
+     behaviour, not just whether the diff still fits.
+   - **Unclear** — keep the patch, but flag it explicitly as *potentially
+     stale* (needs a human or a deeper look) rather than silently re-carrying
+     it or silently dropping it. Silence in either direction is the failure
+     mode to avoid.
+3. Only after that review, rebase: `git rebase --onto wine-<new-tag>
+   wine-<old-tag> patch/<topic>`.
+4. Rebuild `kasm/main` from the new tag + reviewed topic branches, bump the
+   `-pN` tag (starts back at `p1` on a new upstream base).
+5. Record the outcome — kept/ported, dropped-because-absorbed, or
+   flagged-as-stale, per topic branch — in wine-assess's
+   `docs/wine-fork.md` (the authoritative ledger; see below), then push the
+   updated branches and the new `kasm-wine-<tag>-pN` tag to `origin`.
+
+## Current patch set (orientation only — see wine-fork.md for the live ledger)
+
+Base `wine-11.16` (upstream tag `8da89f8493b`), tagged `kasm-wine-11.16-p1`. 29
+patches across 5 topic branches, all sourced from `ryzendew/Affinity-Wine-Builder`
+`patches/wine-11.12/` (the ElementalWarrior Affinity lineage), imported at
+`wine-11.12` and rebased onto `11.16`:
+
+| Topic branch | Carries |
+| --- | --- |
+| `patch/dxcore-elementalwarrior` | dxcore adapter-enumerating implementation |
+| `patch/opencl-elementalwarrior` | OpenCL ICD thunks + caches |
+| `patch/vkd3d-shader-affinity` | vkd3d-shader output-signature/SPIR-V/GLSL fixes |
+| `patch/d2d1-geometry-fixes` | d2d1 bezier/outline geometry fixes |
+| `patch/comdlg32-xdg-portal` | native file dialogs via XDG Desktop Portal (inert without a portal bus; kept for now) |
+
+Full per-commit detail, port notes, and verification history: wine-assess's
+`docs/wine-fork.md`.
+
+## Where the authoritative record lives
+
+**This file is orientation; it is not the ledger.** The live, per-cycle record
+of what's carried, what was dropped, and why is `docs/wine-fork.md` in
+`~/dev/kasm/projects/wine-assess`, because that's the project that consumes
+this fork and tracks why each patch set exists. Update that file, not this
+one, when a patch set changes. Also relevant there:
+
+- `docs/wine-gap-discovery.md` — how a wine-assess run turns "no lever fixes
+  this" into a candidate patch for this fork (the `wine-gap` escalation class).
+- `OUTSTANDING_ISSUES.md` — tracked items against this fork, e.g. `OI-027`
+  (this fork's own existence and hosting), `OI-031` (the gap-discovery
+  pipeline that will eventually feed it patches), `OI-038` (a candidate found
+  2026-09-13 — WinMerge's menu bar — not yet pinned to a specific call, so
+  **not** a patch yet; an example of the "flag, don't guess" discipline this
+  file asks for).
+
+## Open items
+
+- `containers/wine-kasm/` in wine-assess builds `kasm/main` from an **exported
+  source tree**, not this repo directly — the image doesn't need `.git`
+  history, just the tree at the tag.
